<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3 id="screen">Why the screen behaves the way it does.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    The screen is a 1.54&Prime; e-paper panel, 200&times;200 pixels. It keeps its last
    image with the power off and uses no power to keep it there. That is why
    the wordmark shows that the device is off, and why the screen should be blank for
    storage.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    In flight, each image uses a partial refresh. A full refresh, which
    flashes the screen black and white for a second and a half, happens twice in
    a session: at power on, and at power off. A page change uses a
    single black wipe instead, and the alarm&rsquo;s one flash a second is the
    panel&rsquo;s own limit rather than a rate we picked. Faster refreshes are possible,
    and repeatedly switching a large black area that fast permanently marks the screen.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Every image uses a partial refresh</li>
    <li>The screen refreshes fully twice a session</li>
    <li>A page change is one black wipe, a third of a second</li>
    <li>An image identical to the last is never sent</li>
    <li>The screen is left alone above 50&nbsp;&deg;C, its maker&rsquo;s limit</li>
    <li>The screen is parked white at the cutoff, never mid-picture</li>
  </ul>
</section>

